Docker Compose is a tool that lets you define and run a multi-container application using a single YAML file, instead of typing a long `docker run` command for every 
container. You describe all your services (frontend, backend, database, etc.) in one `compose.yaml` file, and then a single command starts everything together .

A Compose file is organized around three main top-level sections :
Section	                              What it defines                                                                                                 	Example
services   	The containers that make up your app. Each service has a name, an image (or a build path), ports, environment variables, and volumes.   	web, db, api
networks	  Virtual networks that connect services. Services on the same network can reach each other by service name (Docker's built-in DNS).	    front-tier, back-tier
volumes	    Persistent storage that survives container restarts. Defined at the top level, then attached to a service.                                  	db-data

 Essential Commands
Command	                                              What it does
docker compose up                             	Creates and starts all services (attached). Add -d for background .
docker compose up --build	                      Rebuilds images before starting (use after changing a Dockerfile or code) .
docker compose down	                            Stops and removes containers and networks. Volumes survive .
docker compose down -v	                        Also deletes named volumes — data loss .
docker compose ps	                              Shows the status of running services .
docker compose logs -f      	                  Streams logs from all services (or name one: logs -f backend) .
docker compose exec <service> <cmd>	            Runs a command inside a running container .

Here's a minimal example with a frontend and a backend:

``` a simple yaml file
services:
  yt-dlp-ubuntu:
    image: <username>/<repositoryname>
    build: .
```

FOR YAML FILES: The Four Automatic Default Filenames
When you run docker compose up without specifying a file, Docker automatically looks in your current directory for one of four filenames in this exact order of preference:
compose.yaml (Current official preferred standard), compose.yml, docker-compose.yaml (Legacy name, kept for backwards compatibility),  
docker-compose.yml (Legacy name, kept for backwards compatibility)  #(Note: .yaml and .yml are the exact same file format; 
.yaml is just the official extension recommended by the YAML specification.)If both compose.yaml and docker-compose.yaml exist in the same folder,
Docker will prioritize compose.yaml.

- You can name your file anything you want—such as compose.dev.yaml, compose.prod.yaml, or my-app.yaml. Because Docker won't detect custom names automatically,
you simply pass the -f (--file) flag when running commands:   *docker compose -f compose.prod.yaml up -d
